# Huabu

> A canvas-based framework for human–AI collaboration.

Huabu provides a shared two-dimensional workspace where ideas, notes, documents,
questions, and AI outputs live side by side as **persistent nodes**. Instead of
treating AI as a chat window detached from your work, Huabu places the human and
the AI inside the same canvas so they can build on each other's contributions
over time.

This repository (`sediment`) is the reference implementation of Huabu.

---

## Two Principles

### 1. Externalize Thinking

Huabu makes the intermediate structure of thought visible on the canvas.
Ideas can be organized, revised, and revisited outside of working memory.
The canvas captures not only **content**, but also **structure** — spatial
arrangement reflects relationships, priorities, uncertainty, and the emerging
clusters of thought that usually stay implicit in a linear document or chat.

### 2. Share Cognitive Space

Because the AI operates in the same workspace as the human, it can access the broader context of the work rather than only the latest instruction. This lets
it:

- help organize materials,
- synthesize across nodes,
- identify what remains unresolved,
- and support the development of ideas into more structured and actionable
  outputs.

The canvas is the shared memory; both sides read from it and write to it.

---

## What's in a Canvas

A Huabu canvas is an infinite 2D surface composed of typed, persistent nodes
that the user and the AI can both create, modify, link, and group.

| Node type   | Purpose                                                       |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Note        | Rich-text / Markdown notes for ideas and drafts               |
| Text        | Lightweight text blocks for labels and annotations            |
| Web         | Embedded web pages as living references                       |
| PDF         | PDF reader with highlighting that flows back into the canvas  |
| Image/Video | Visual material kept next to the thinking it informs          |
| Frame       | Grouping container that gives a region of the canvas identity |
| Edge        | Explicit relationships between nodes                          |

Around these primitives Huabu adds:

- **Intent system** — press `Ctrl/Cmd+I` and the AI proposes the next useful
  moves based on what is currently on the canvas.
- **Auto layout** — semantic, force-directed arrangement so the canvas stays
  legible as it grows.
- **Per-canvas node content storage** — each canvas owns its node content
  locally, so the AI can read and write directly against the same material
  the user is working on without a separate indexing step.
- **AI chat side panel** — a conversational surface whose actions land back
  on the canvas as nodes and edges, not just as text replies.

See the user guide in [docs/user-guide](./docs/user-guide/README.md) for
details.

---

## Repository Layout

This is a pnpm workspace.

```
apps/
  web/      React + Vite canvas client
  server/   Fastify backend: agent, canvas, storage, preprocessing, artifact
  data/     Local working data (sqlite, vault, artifacts)
packages/
  shared/   Shared types and utilities
docs/       Design notes and user guide
```

Key backend modules ([apps/server/src/modules](apps/server/src/modules)):

- `agent/` — the AI agent runtime that reads and writes the canvas
- `canvas/` — canvas state, commands, and persistence
- `storage/` — per-canvas node content storage
- `preprocessing/` — ingestion pipeline (e.g. PDF → structured content)
- `artifact/` — generated artifacts produced by the agent
- `web/` — web fetching utilities used by node ingestion

Key frontend areas ([apps/web/src](apps/web/src)):

- `components/Nodes/` — node implementations
- `components/Panels/` — side panels including the AI chat
- `handler/canvasCommand/` — canvas mutation commands shared with the agent
- `handler/autoLayout/` — semantic auto-layout
- `hooks/useAgentStream.ts` — streaming agent protocol on the client

---

## Getting Started

Requirements: Node.js 20+ and pnpm 10+.

```bash
pnpm install
pnpm dev
```

This starts the server, the web client, and the shared package in watch mode.
You can also start them individually:

```bash
pnpm dev:server
pnpm dev:web
pnpm dev:shared
```

Other useful scripts:

```bash
pnpm typecheck   # type-check every workspace package
pnpm lint        # lint everything
pnpm lint:fix    # lint and auto-fix
pnpm format      # prettier write
```

For environment setup details, see [docs/setup.md](./docs/setup.md).

---

## Connecting external coding agents

Sediment can talk to AI coding agents that already run on your machine —
**GitHub Copilot CLI**, **Claude Code**, **Gemini CLI**, and others that
speak the [Agent Client Protocol](https://agentclientprotocol.com). Once
connected, the agent shows up inside Sediment and can read and modify
files in whatever project folder you pointed it at.

### One-time setup

1. With `pnpm dev` running, open Sediment in your browser, click the
   **gear icon** (top-right), and open the **External Agents** section.
   Click **Generate code** — Sediment will show you a short pairing
   code (e.g. `XXXX-XXXX`) that is valid for 60 seconds. Copy it; you
   will paste it into the agentlet command in the next step.

   > Pairing codes are **ephemeral**. The first `bin/agentlet` that
   > successfully hellos with the code claims it for that agent — after
   > that the code stays valid for reconnects (wifi blips, dev hot-
   > reloads) but cannot be used to attach a different agent. Closing
   > agentlet gracefully or clicking the ✕ next to the code in the UI
   > revokes it immediately; the server holds no persisted token on
   > disk. Setting `ENABLE_ACP` / `ACP_DEV_TOKEN` in `.env` has no
   > effect.

2. _Optional._ Make the `agentlet` command available from any folder.
   Run the snippet that matches your shell from the project root:

   ```bash
   # bash
   echo 'export PATH="'"$PWD"'/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.bashrc && exec bash
   ```

   ```bash
   # zsh (macOS default)
   echo 'export PATH="'"$PWD"'/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.zshrc && exec zsh
   ```

   ```fish
   # fish
   fish_add_path "$PWD/bin"
   ```

   ```powershell
   # PowerShell (Windows) — persists for the current user
   [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(
     'Path',
     "$((Get-Location).Path)\bin;$([Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable('Path','User'))",
     'User')
   ```

   If you skip this, just use the full path everywhere
   (e.g. `~/Sediment/bin/agentlet …` or `.\bin\agentlet …` on Windows).

### Connecting an agent

With `pnpm dev` running, open another terminal, `cd` into the project
folder you want the agent to work on, generate a fresh pairing code in
the Settings UI, and start the agent of your choice (replace
`XXXX-XXXX` with the code Sediment just gave you):

```bash
# GitHub Copilot CLI
agentlet --token XXXX-XXXX --agent "copilot --acp"

# Claude Code
agentlet --token XXXX-XXXX --agent "claude --acp"

# Gemini CLI
agentlet --token XXXX-XXXX --agent "gemini --acp"
```

> You can also `export AGENTLET_TOKEN=XXXX-XXXX` before launching and
> drop the `--token` flag. The wrapper will refuse to start without
> one or the other.

That's it — the agent will show up in Sediment's external-agent panel and
you can hand it tasks from any canvas. Reconnect / re-launch attempts
from the **same** agentlet keep working with the same code; spinning
up a second agent requires a fresh code.

### Tool-permission prompts

Some agents pause to ask for permission before running a tool that edits
files, executes commands, etc. When they do, the request surfaces **inline
in the chat** as a permission card, and the agent waits until you click
**Allow** or **Reject** (a request times out and is treated as cancelled
after 5 minutes).

Whether you ever see these prompts is up to the agent, not Sediment:

- `copilot --acp --allow-all` — the `--allow-all` flag tells Copilot to
  **auto-approve everything on its side**, so it never sends a permission
  request and no card appears. Drop `--allow-all` if you want to approve
  each sensitive action yourself.
- `claude --acp` / `gemini --acp` — these keep their own default policy and
  will ask before risky operations (typically `edit` / `execute`).

> Permission decisions are transient — they are not persisted and do not
> appear in canvas history after a refresh.

### Updating the bundled `agentlet`

`external/agentlet/` is a snapshot of
[`hai-team/agentlet`](https://github.com/hai-team/agentlet). Maintainers
sync it with `pnpm run subtree:pull:agentlet` (pull upstream changes) or
`pnpm run subtree:push:agentlet` (publish local changes back).

